<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Principality of Lucca and Piombino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Principality of Lucca and Piombino was a Napoleonic state in Tuscany created in 1805 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa Bonaparte</a> and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felice-baciocchi">Felice Baciocchi</a>. It replaced the old Republic of Lucca and absorbed the Principality of Piombino. Elisa reformed its administration and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> marble, and the state ended with Napoleon's fall in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/principality-of-lucca-and-piombino.jpg" alt="Principality of Lucca and Piombino" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hierakares · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Principality_of_Lucca_(1805-1809).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The principality joined two territories. The new Principality of Lucca covered the lands of the old republic, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had occupied since late 1799. The Principality of Piombino had been occupied in 1804 and given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> to his sister Elisa in March 1805, with the title of Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portoferraio">Portoferraio</a>. From July 1805 the two were ruled as a single monarchy and a client state of Napoleonic France.</p><p>Elisa held the real power. Her husband, Felice Pasquale Baciocchi, was formally made Prince of Lucca at the request of the people, who hoped to keep at least a semblance of independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1806 the lands of the former Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> and the former Este Garfagnana were added.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> himself wrote the constitution on 22 June 1805, setting up a Council of State to advise the prince and a legislative Senate. The French franc was the currency, and only a few local coins were struck. On 3 March 1809 Napoleon made Elisa Grand Duchess of Tuscany, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> over a region annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> two years earlier from the former Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>. Lucca and Piombino then became part of the grand duchy and so a territory of the French Empire, with a special status and a prefect, Antoine-Marie-Pierre de Hautmesnil, though never a French department.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1814 Austrian troops occupied Lucca, ending French rule as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> fell. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave Piombino to the Grand Duchy of Tuscany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> to the exiled Napoleon. Lucca became a state again as the Duchy of Lucca, granted to Maria Louisa, Infanta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and former Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, in place of Parma, which went to Napoleon's wife, Marie-Louise. She accepted only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1815">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1817 promised Parma to her son Charles Louis after Marie-Louise's death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Principality of Lucca and Piombino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
